      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Quantization Mechanics: W4A16 vs FP16 Precision Loss and Perplexity Trade-Offs
      </h2>
      <p>
        To compress a 360-million parameter model into 118MB without catastrophic cognitive collapse, Transformers.js v3 utilizes <strong>W4A16 quantization</strong> (4-bit weight integers with float16 scale factors and activations).
      </p>
      <p>
        Traditional uniform 4-bit integer quantization (INT4) frequently suffers from outlier clipping in attention projection matrices, introducing perplexity spikes. Under W4A16, weights are grouped into sub-blocks of 32 or 128 elements, each normalized by a dedicated float16 scale factor. On the WikiText-2 validation dataset:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>FP16 Baseline Perplexity</strong>: 9.82.</li>
        <li><strong>q4f16 Quantized Perplexity</strong>: 10.14 (an imperceptible 3.2% degradation in perplexity for an 83.5% reduction in memory footprint).</li>
        <li><strong>Uniform INT4 Perplexity</strong>: 14.65 (severe loss of syntactic coherence).</li>
      </ul>

