      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Top 4 WebGPU In-Browser Failure Modes &amp; Diagnostic Troubleshooting Runbook
      </h2>
      <p>
        When building client-side neural interfaces for production web applications, implement defensive guards against the following 4 common browser failure modes:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-4 text-slate-300">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">1. Error: "navigator.gpu is undefined":</strong> Occurs when the user's browser lacks WebGPU support (older Firefox or iOS &lt; 17.4) or when WebGPU is disabled via hardware acceleration flags. <em>Remediation:</em> Check <code>if (!navigator.gpu)</code> during page boot and provide a graceful fallback to a WASM SIMD execution provider or server API proxy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">2. GPU Device Lost (Context Loss):</strong> If the user switches display outputs, minimizes the browser tab, or their system encounters extreme VRAM pressure, the browser kills the WebGPU device context. <em>Remediation:</em> Listen for the <code>device.lost</code> event and trigger an automatic re-initialization pipeline to rebuild GPU tensor buffers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">3. SharedArrayBuffer / Cross-Origin Isolation Errors:</strong> If hosting multi-threaded WASM workers alongside WebGPU, browsers require specific security headers. <em>Remediation:</em> Ensure your web server dispatches <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin</code> and <code>Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">4. Out-Of-Memory (OOM) on Extended Contexts:</strong> When generating over 3,000 tokens on a mobile device, dynamic KV cache buffer expansion can exceed device buffer limits. <em>Remediation:</em> Enforce strict sliding window attention or cap <code>max_new_tokens</code> to 512 on mobile user agents.
        </li>
      </ul>

